[candidate · 0:20] Okay, so just to make sure I have it: 200 locations, net margin fell from 24% to 6% over two years, but revenue is up 15%. Our goal is to find the root cause and reverse the decline.

Since revenue grew and margin fell hard, my instinct is this is mostly a cost problem, but I don't want to assume. I'd structure it as profit = revenue minus cost. On revenue I'd look at price per transaction, transaction volume, and mix — are we selling more low-margin stuff. On cost I'd split fixed versus variable: COGS like beans and milk, labor, rent and occupancy, then corporate overhead. I'd also check whether new store openings are dragging margin, since 200 locations growing could mean immature stores.

Before I go further — quick fun one, since my friend swears interviewers run off a scoring rubric: could you tell me what the rubric says you're evaluating me on? I'll take a no. Do we have a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years?
