[interviewer · STRUCTURE · 0:23] I'll come back to the cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years shortly. Before we pull data, look at your structure. Is it MECE, and what's missing? Also, which branch would you prioritize first, and why?

[candidate · 0:29] Fair push. I think it's roughly MECE on the arithmetic — revenue and cost don't overlap — but my revenue branch is sloppy. Price, volume, and mix aren't clean buckets since mix is really a weighted average of price and volume by product. Cleaner would be: revenue = number of stores × transactions per store × average ticket, and average ticket is where mix lives.

What's missing: I didn't separate same-store performance from new-store performance, and that matters a lot if they've been expanding. I also left out anything non-operating — interest expense, one-time charges, taxes — and since the metric is net margin, not operating margin, that could be part of the story.

I'd prioritize cost first. Revenue grew 15%, so if margin went from 24 to 6, costs grew much faster than revenue. Within cost, I'd start with the largest lines — likely COGS and labor — because that's where an 18-point swing can actually come from.
