[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 0:59] Right. Let's keep to the case. I don't have transaction volume by year. Beyond pricing, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse the margin decline? Give me as many distinct ideas as you can.

[candidate · 1:07] No volume data, so I'll assume traffic has been roughly stable and revenue growth came from store count.

Beyond price increases, buckets:

Procurement: renegotiate bean contracts, hedge or lock in forward pricing, consolidate suppliers for volume discounts, dual-source dairy and packaging, switch packaging to a cheaper spec.

Product and mix: push higher-margin items — drip coffee, food, retail bags — reformulate drinks to use less of the expensive input, shift customers toward larger sizes where margin is better, reduce SKUs with bad margins.

Waste: measure spoilage and over-pouring. In coffee, portioning discipline and shrink can be a few points.

Revenue per customer without list-price hikes: upsell add-ons, loyalty program, subscriptions, smaller size as a price-anchoring trick.

Overhead: it's up two points — look at corporate headcount and whether new-store support costs are being absorbed centrally.

Footprint: close or renegotiate underperforming locations.

One caveat — I'd want store-level margin variance before cutting anything, since averages could hide a tail of bad stores.
