[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:19] Let's keep to the case. You said a price increase on flat volume would recover roughly 10 to 12 points of margin. Walk me through how you got there. And what's the single biggest risk to your recommendation, and how would you mitigate it?

[candidate · 1:27] Let me actually run it, because I think I was loose.

Index current revenue at 100. Costs: COGS 58, labor 22, overhead 14, total 94, profit 6.

Raise prices 10%, volume flat. Revenue 110. Costs don't change — same cups sold, same inputs — so still 94. Profit is 16 on 110, which is 14.5%. That's an 8.5 point margin gain, not 10 to 12. So I overstated it; thanks for making me check. To get back near 24% I'd need something like a 20% increase: revenue 120, profit 26, margin 21.7%. That's a lot to push through at once, which is why I'd phase it and lean on procurement for the rest.

Biggest risk is elasticity — specialty coffee has substitutes, and a 20% hike could cut traffic enough to wipe out the gain. Mitigation: test in a geographically mixed subset of stores, measure transaction counts weekly, and roll back fast if traffic drops more than a few percent.
